WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.360
Next up, we have our second panel of the day.

00:05.360 --> 00:10.440
The panel is on journalism with Django, which is obviously where Django came from.

00:10.440 --> 00:16.000
So please introduce our panelists, who I believe will then be introducing themselves.

00:16.000 --> 00:25.120
Hello, everybody, once again.

00:25.120 --> 00:33.120
Because Django came out of a journalism environment, probably for that reason, it's had some uptake

00:33.120 --> 00:37.200
in the journalism world, and we thought it would be interesting to get the perspective

00:37.200 --> 00:47.360
of that one type of Django user and how they use it, how they think it can be improved,

00:47.360 --> 00:48.720
and just any other thoughts they have.

00:48.720 --> 00:53.960
So we have a number of illustrious journalists who use Django here, and they're all going

00:53.960 --> 00:55.680
to introduce themselves.

00:55.680 --> 00:57.680
First, Ben.

00:57.680 --> 01:05.240
Hi, my name is Ben Welsh, and I work at the Los Angeles Times, where we do some database

01:05.240 --> 01:10.740
projects for the web, and before that, I worked in D.C. at the Center for Public Integrity

01:10.740 --> 01:15.600
and kind of came into it through the investigative journalism world like Matt here.

01:15.600 --> 01:18.560
I guess that's my introduction.

01:18.560 --> 01:20.280
I'm Matt Waite.

01:20.280 --> 01:25.360
My title is News Technologist at the St. Petersburg Times, which is to say, if anybody

01:25.360 --> 01:30.120
knows what the hell I do, I'd love to know, you can tell me later.

01:30.120 --> 01:34.480
I've used Django for a little over a year now, I've launched two sites.

01:34.480 --> 01:39.400
My first project, still online, is a project called PolitiFact, which fat checks presidential

01:39.400 --> 01:42.400
candidates.

01:42.400 --> 01:43.400
What's the second?

01:43.400 --> 01:44.400
I'm sorry?

01:44.400 --> 01:45.400
What's the second project you've done?

01:45.400 --> 01:50.200
Oh, it was a, we just launched it, it was a real estate, neighborhood-based real estate

01:50.200 --> 01:55.480
site, where you can, if you live in the Tampa Bay area, you can get a somewhat ridiculous

01:55.480 --> 02:00.400
amount of detail about how houses are selling and for how much in your very small micro

02:00.400 --> 02:01.400
neighborhood.

02:01.400 --> 02:02.400
Cool.

02:02.400 --> 02:07.000
And Ben, you didn't mention any projects that you'd worked on, so could you just, really

02:07.000 --> 02:08.000
quickly?

02:08.000 --> 02:12.040
We've only been doing Django at the LA Times for, since I think about Memorial Day, and

02:12.040 --> 02:14.320
we've put out three apps since then.

02:14.320 --> 02:19.440
The first was, we ripped off one of yours, we did a Memorial Day thing about California

02:19.440 --> 02:22.960
servicemen and women who died in Iraq and Afghanistan.

02:22.960 --> 02:27.440
We also did dog registrations in Los Angeles, so you could see the most common dogs or look

02:27.440 --> 02:30.960
up your name and see where they're at and what the most common breeds are, goofy stuff

02:30.960 --> 02:32.600
like that.

02:32.600 --> 02:37.040
And then this week, we put out, on a very tight deadline, I'm proud to report, the

02:37.040 --> 02:41.840
new school scores here in the state of California, so you could see how your local school's doing

02:41.840 --> 02:43.680
or compare them across the state.

02:43.680 --> 02:44.680
Cool.

02:44.920 --> 02:49.800
My name is Maura Chase, and I work at the Atlanta Journal of Constitution, which, if

02:49.800 --> 02:55.320
you don't know, that was actually where Adrian worked first, but that's another story.

02:55.320 --> 02:59.800
I've worked there for about a year, and I've been using Django for about two years.

02:59.800 --> 03:04.780
We don't have, we have a kind of a separate CMS, we don't have Ellington or anything,

03:04.780 --> 03:09.720
so we build more kind of like site enhancements and special projects in Django.

03:09.720 --> 03:13.680
Probably our biggest thing is we do all the photo galleries on the site, they get lots

03:13.680 --> 03:18.960
of traffic, people love to look at photos.

03:18.960 --> 03:23.680
And I'm Matt Croydon, I'm development manager at Media for Media, which is the shiny new

03:23.680 --> 03:25.160
name for World Online.

03:25.160 --> 03:31.240
You know, obviously we're associated with the journal World, birthplace of Django, and

03:31.240 --> 03:35.160
I've been involved a lot with both the development of Ellington, our content management system,

03:35.160 --> 03:42.080
and also a lot of database journalism, data visualization projects for and with the newsroom.

03:43.080 --> 03:48.240
All right, so as I mentioned at the beginning, we're putting this together, putting this

03:48.240 --> 03:51.720
panel together because Django came out of this journalism environment.

03:51.720 --> 03:53.960
I wanted to get your thoughts.

03:53.960 --> 03:59.560
Are you using Django because it came out of a journalism environment, or would you have

03:59.560 --> 04:01.280
found it otherwise?

04:01.280 --> 04:05.000
Do we have like extra cred in that world, in your opinion?

04:05.000 --> 04:06.000
Does it matter?

04:06.000 --> 04:07.000
How does it help us?

04:07.000 --> 04:08.000
How does it hurt us?

04:08.000 --> 04:10.200
What do you think?

04:10.320 --> 04:12.880
I don't think I should be answering this, so I'm going to pass it off.

04:12.880 --> 04:16.240
Yeah, Matt doesn't really have a choice.

04:16.240 --> 04:20.280
I think that I actually used Django before I worked at the paper.

04:20.280 --> 04:24.920
So I came from Django from a non kind of paper background.

04:24.920 --> 04:27.920
I think that, I mean, kind of both.

04:27.920 --> 04:32.680
It definitely has extra credit, because it came out of newspapers, and everyone knows

04:32.680 --> 04:38.080
about Django and they know about the Lawrence Journal world, so there's obviously great

04:38.080 --> 04:43.560
reasons to use Django besides that fact, but I think that's definitely a big deal.

04:43.560 --> 04:44.560
I agree.

04:44.560 --> 04:45.560
I think it's evolving.

04:45.560 --> 04:46.560
I think it is both.

04:46.560 --> 04:50.680
I think it started out as, hey, some other newspaper made this, let's check it out.

04:50.680 --> 04:55.680
I joke that the reason that the newspaper people use Django is slug fields, because

04:55.680 --> 04:57.400
it harkens back to hot type.

04:57.400 --> 05:02.800
I mean, when people set type and put it there were slugs that they use, so that's like comfort

05:02.800 --> 05:03.800
food to us.

05:03.800 --> 05:05.640
Oh, look, slug's weed.

05:06.160 --> 05:11.800
I have seen, just to interrupt, I have seen an uptake in usage and awareness of the term

05:11.800 --> 05:16.160
slug, like since Django has been released in random blogs, but...

05:16.160 --> 05:19.000
It's like home cooking.

05:19.000 --> 05:22.840
Can we swap in TK for null at some point?

05:22.840 --> 05:25.520
That one's before my time.

05:25.520 --> 05:26.520
Long story.

05:26.520 --> 05:30.880
But I would say that the answer to that is a resounding yes, and it may not necessarily

05:30.880 --> 05:33.120
always be for the best reasons.

05:33.120 --> 05:34.280
How do ideas spread?

05:34.280 --> 05:35.280
How do memes happen?

05:35.280 --> 05:40.520
I mean, there's a degree to which that there's, it's kind of a bad phrase to use, but almost

05:40.520 --> 05:44.240
a cult of personality that kind of happens to a certain degree, where within the journalism

05:44.240 --> 05:48.640
world there's this knowledge among managers and among people who run the organizations

05:48.640 --> 05:53.200
that they need to change, but a lot of them don't really know how, or what that change

05:53.200 --> 05:55.400
should look like, or how it should take shape.

05:55.400 --> 06:00.720
And I think when there are people within the journalism community who are at least attempting

06:00.720 --> 06:05.680
to articulate some version of an alternative or a future, I think that it has more credibility

06:05.680 --> 06:06.960
than it would comes for outside.

06:06.960 --> 06:12.800
So I think when Adrian or Rob Curley or whoever shows up on Romanesco saying, this is one

06:12.800 --> 06:17.400
version of the future, or what I think a possibility for the future could be, I think that that

06:17.400 --> 06:23.360
carries a lot more credibility than, or it just has a greater chance of propagating itself.

06:23.360 --> 06:26.200
Another thing that I would mention, and we'll probably talk about this more in a little

06:26.200 --> 06:32.520
bit, the admin, the auto admin, is just a killer feature for a newsroom because of deadlines

06:32.520 --> 06:36.920
and it's there, and you get people putting in data right away for free.

06:36.920 --> 06:38.720
So that's a, yeah, go ahead.

06:38.720 --> 06:39.720
Deadlines in a matter of hours.

06:39.720 --> 06:43.040
I mean, yeah, the admin makes that, what I was going to say, when they get the reels

06:43.040 --> 06:46.920
that go into Ben's point, is that newspaper editors tend to be more fad conscious than

06:46.920 --> 06:48.800
a gang of tweens at a mall.

06:48.800 --> 06:53.080
So one person makes something work, they're going to chase it like a herd of kids.

06:54.080 --> 06:58.040
I saw an amazing slide at a presentation at another conference where it was a picture

06:58.040 --> 07:02.520
of six year olds playing soccer, and they all bunched around the ball and they just

07:02.520 --> 07:06.320
chased the ball around like this little mass of kids just chasing the ball around the field.

07:06.320 --> 07:07.320
Journalism.

07:07.320 --> 07:08.320
Yeah.

07:08.320 --> 07:09.320
It's pretty much right there.

07:09.320 --> 07:15.080
But I mean, and that's flip, and I in a sense agree with it, but I mean that also is an

07:15.080 --> 07:19.800
opportunity for everybody in this room to do something because you have an audience

07:19.800 --> 07:26.160
of potential customers who will give you money to do things for them and to come up

07:26.160 --> 07:31.000
with cool stuff and also be able to put that on a platform where a lot of people will see

07:31.000 --> 07:33.680
it, which is, I mean, that's a lot of fun.

07:33.680 --> 07:38.040
And so there's opportunities for, I think, products made out of Django or made from Django

07:38.040 --> 07:43.040
or I don't know what, to be sold to the newspaper audience because there's competitor products

07:43.040 --> 07:47.600
that are being sold now that suck and you guys could kick them, you guys could take

07:47.600 --> 07:48.600
over.

07:48.600 --> 07:49.600
Like Caspio.

07:49.600 --> 08:01.400
So, Matt, what was the quickest turnaround ever for a journalism Django project that

08:01.400 --> 08:02.880
you'd made?

08:02.880 --> 08:05.360
Four hours.

08:05.360 --> 08:10.760
When Hurricane Fay looked like it was going to come straight up our tailpipe, I noticed

08:10.760 --> 08:16.200
that what the newsroom was doing was taking all of this structured information and just

08:16.200 --> 08:20.480
dumping it in a blog and they were doing it at such a volume that important information

08:20.480 --> 08:25.720
like which hurricane evacuation shelters were open was rocketing down the list.

08:25.720 --> 08:29.480
I mean, this is a chronological order blog.

08:29.480 --> 08:30.680
These shelters are opening.

08:30.680 --> 08:37.360
That's a life or death thing and in half an hour, an hour, it's six posts down the line

08:37.360 --> 08:39.960
and it's gone and you can't find it anymore.

08:39.960 --> 08:45.040
So that bothered me, so I sat down and just quick banged out an app in Geojango actually

08:45.040 --> 08:48.000
that mapped out where the hurricane evacuation shelters were so you could see where they

08:48.000 --> 08:53.160
were and which one was the closest one to you and the admin allowed the clerks to just

08:53.160 --> 08:56.880
start punching in the data and turned out that Fay turned away from us and we never

08:56.880 --> 08:57.880
got to use it.

08:57.880 --> 09:02.800
So, yeah, I built one in four hours that nobody ever saw.

09:02.800 --> 09:03.800
The other Matt?

09:03.800 --> 09:09.680
I mean, for the longest time, we had the sort of running gag at work of, you know, we'd

09:09.680 --> 09:14.840
talk about an idea over lunch and by the end of the day, it was launched and, I mean,

09:14.840 --> 09:19.400
that's pretty common for both the, you know, short turnaround stuff staying up all night

09:19.400 --> 09:24.280
to get something done for the next day's paper or the next day's news cycle but also just,

09:24.280 --> 09:28.800
you know, trivial little things, hey, this would be fantastic, let's do it, oh, it's

09:28.800 --> 09:30.960
done by the end of the day.

09:30.960 --> 09:32.960
Any particular examples?

09:32.960 --> 09:36.280
Sorry, I'm blanking.

09:36.280 --> 09:38.840
No problem at all.

09:39.200 --> 09:46.000
Have you guys generally found that the workflow is create your models, stick some reporters

09:46.000 --> 09:50.360
loose on the admin and then they're putting in data while you're working on the views

09:50.360 --> 09:51.960
and the templates?

09:51.960 --> 09:54.480
Yeah, definitely.

09:54.480 --> 09:59.720
It seems like at the general world, we're sort of shifting from, you know, here's my

09:59.720 --> 10:07.400
data, let's model it in Django apps and, you know, unleash the reporter in the admin.

10:07.400 --> 10:12.120
These days, we're doing a lot more, you know, looking at the structured data ahead of time,

10:12.120 --> 10:16.920
you know, get it in a Google Docs or, you know, so that we can get to it via CSV and

10:16.920 --> 10:21.360
then doing, you know, automated imports or, you know, providing tools for if it's just

10:21.360 --> 10:26.600
a real simple, you know, tabular or, you know, one thing over time, you know, we have tools

10:26.600 --> 10:32.640
that don't necessarily require as much programmer intervention as it used to.

10:33.360 --> 10:34.600
Right, right.

10:34.600 --> 10:37.440
Ben, how are things at the LA Times?

10:37.440 --> 10:43.360
Well, I think the admin came in really handy when we did the Memorial Day project about

10:43.360 --> 10:48.160
casualties in the war where we hadn't done any Django projects before and it was sort

10:48.160 --> 10:52.760
of I had to sell people on letting us do this and one of the things that made me comfortable

10:52.760 --> 10:57.280
that we could do it on a tight deadline in a couple of maybe three weeks when it required

10:57.280 --> 11:01.960
a lot of additional entry and modification of the source data was that I knew that on

11:01.960 --> 11:06.120
day one, once I had the models up, I could have someone totally independent of me doing

11:06.120 --> 11:10.800
all that work while the web development was happening and that is, I mean, just a huge

11:10.800 --> 11:11.800
advantage.

11:11.800 --> 11:16.240
I mean, I'm sure people here in other circumstances are familiar with that advantage but, I mean,

11:16.240 --> 11:20.120
it's something that is just peculiarly suited to a newsroom, you know, when things just

11:20.120 --> 11:24.600
come up quickly and you have to do it, you know, if there was just umpteen examples of

11:24.600 --> 11:29.120
how you could work with it but I agree.

11:29.120 --> 11:39.600
So journalists are probably some of the stupidest people technologically who use Django admins.

11:39.600 --> 11:43.480
Are they taping this?

11:43.480 --> 11:50.600
So I'm wondering what your experience has been in showing journalists and reporters

11:50.600 --> 11:52.920
the admin just in general.

11:52.920 --> 11:53.920
How does it go?

11:53.920 --> 11:56.920
We'll go over with them.

11:56.920 --> 12:00.800
On that note, one of my favorite jokes is both Matt and I come out of the investigative

12:00.800 --> 12:05.000
journalism world where computers were adopted in like the late 70s and early 80s by some

12:05.000 --> 12:08.120
forward thinking guys who just wanted to do more sophisticated analysis.

12:08.120 --> 12:12.560
So you want to do like some social science, like heavy duty, like let's get at the data

12:12.560 --> 12:14.680
from the local courthouse or whatever.

12:14.680 --> 12:17.480
They learned how to do it and both of us kind of came up trained in that world which kind

12:17.480 --> 12:19.200
of naturally bridged into this.

12:19.200 --> 12:22.920
And the really awkward phrase for people to do that which just sounds very antiquated

12:22.920 --> 12:28.320
today but is a computer assisted reporting which is you know, and I always make the joke

12:28.320 --> 12:31.480
that it's still around today and it's still used very frequently in our world and it's

12:31.480 --> 12:34.880
almost kind of embarrassing for us, at least for me it is, because I always think you never

12:34.880 --> 12:39.680
hear today of a computer assisted photographer or a computer assisted architect.

12:39.680 --> 12:43.400
You know, it's only in the field of journalism where you still manage to distinguish yourself

12:43.400 --> 12:52.200
by using Excel but Django has made inroads just in the short period of time we've used

12:52.200 --> 12:54.080
it with people who aren't necessarily that savvy.

12:54.080 --> 12:59.600
I mean, it's easier to use as an admin than our CMS or for people who approve comments

12:59.600 --> 13:02.000
on our sites they use it and they all seem to like it.

13:02.000 --> 13:06.760
And one of my things I'm most proud of or is just so great is one of the Metro editor

13:06.760 --> 13:12.720
who I worked with on the Worded thing is not someone who is not very webby but who's open

13:12.720 --> 13:16.960
to change and who wants to do things and who really has been I think rewarded by the process

13:16.960 --> 13:17.960
of this project.

13:17.960 --> 13:21.840
And she came up to me the other day and she said, you know, I'm really looking for something

13:21.840 --> 13:25.320
else for us to jangify, you know?

13:25.320 --> 13:27.160
And to me that just felt like a huge victory.

13:27.160 --> 13:33.440
Yeah, I think the admin feels sort of immediately at home for anyone who's spent any time on

13:33.440 --> 13:38.720
the internet and so that at our organization the only time that we run into trouble with,

13:38.720 --> 13:42.120
you know, this is the admin, this is what it does as someone who's just completely unfamiliar

13:42.120 --> 13:45.040
with computers or has a very limited experience with them.

13:45.040 --> 13:50.040
So anybody else, it's just like riding a bike.

13:50.040 --> 13:53.480
When it comes to the admin, I mean, the greatest thing that ever happened for users of the

13:53.480 --> 13:54.480
admin is like Hotmail.

13:54.480 --> 13:59.960
I tell people if you've used a web-based email account, you can use the admin.

13:59.960 --> 14:01.320
You will survive.

14:01.320 --> 14:07.000
It's just a couple of boxes and you hit save and you're done.

14:07.000 --> 14:10.520
I have never, I haven't had a single problem with anybody who went, oh, I don't know what

14:10.520 --> 14:11.520
I'm doing.

14:11.520 --> 14:12.520
I don't know what I'm doing.

14:12.520 --> 14:18.360
I mean, the web is so permutated society now that just a simple form, you know, people

14:18.360 --> 14:22.280
run into them all the time and that's pretty much what the admin is, is a simple form.

14:22.280 --> 14:26.520
And you actually, as far as how it could be improved, you actually find people asking

14:26.520 --> 14:30.680
for improvements similar to things they see on more advanced web applications like they'll

14:30.680 --> 14:36.160
say, gee, could I moderate all these comments in a batch like from the main screen or, you

14:36.160 --> 14:40.400
know, when you're dealing with the inline changes and all that which I haven't had a

14:40.400 --> 14:45.120
chance to work with 1.0 much yet but I'm told that there's improvements there.

14:45.120 --> 14:51.520
I think we have, you know, a CMS and the Django admin is a lot easier to use than our

14:51.520 --> 14:52.520
paper CMS.

14:52.520 --> 14:54.960
So people generally like it.

14:54.960 --> 14:58.080
One of the things that I think that Django developers don't always do is even before

14:58.080 --> 15:03.640
new forms admin, there's a lot of customizations that you can do to make it easier for people

15:03.640 --> 15:09.240
to enter things, just grouping fields, help text, things like that.

15:09.240 --> 15:14.960
So Maura, before we got on stage, you were talking about how you did some training of

15:14.960 --> 15:15.960
the admin?

15:15.960 --> 15:16.960
Oh, no.

15:16.960 --> 15:21.880
I actually haven't done this yet but my idea was to do a screencast or some training and

15:21.880 --> 15:26.960
just basically talk about the Django admin fields apart from any particular app, just,

15:26.960 --> 15:31.960
you know, these are the different fields that you'll encounter, you know, a foreign key,

15:31.960 --> 15:33.520
you know, many to many.

15:33.520 --> 15:36.760
If it's bold, then it means it's required, if it's not bold, it's not required, that

15:36.760 --> 15:37.760
kind of thing.

15:37.760 --> 15:38.760
Right.

15:38.780 --> 15:44.320
I'm wondering whether we should somehow package that up in a generic fashion, like the, here's

15:44.320 --> 15:49.920
this, like a, you know, walk through one page at a time, here is a required field that's

15:49.920 --> 15:50.920
in bold.

15:50.920 --> 15:51.920
Boom.

15:51.920 --> 15:52.920
And maybe it's like customized for your data.

15:52.920 --> 15:53.920
Right.

15:53.920 --> 15:54.920
Do you think that would be useful?

15:54.920 --> 15:56.200
I think that would be incredibly useful.

15:56.200 --> 16:00.720
I think that because I'm so familiar with the Django admin, I just, there's probably

16:00.720 --> 16:03.520
a lot of things that you kind of take for granted that you already know when you look

16:03.520 --> 16:06.160
at it that maybe someone new to it, it's not as obvious to them.

16:06.160 --> 16:11.000
If you teach them, it's like teaching them to fish, kind of, instead of, you know, giving

16:11.000 --> 16:13.440
them food.

16:13.440 --> 16:20.400
What are the top admin features that you'd like to see?

16:20.400 --> 16:22.480
Other than multiple deletion.

16:22.480 --> 16:25.840
Oh, damn it.

16:25.840 --> 16:26.840
It's perfect.

16:26.840 --> 16:27.840
All right.

16:27.840 --> 16:28.840
Next question.

16:28.840 --> 16:33.080
Well, I kind of bumbled on this in our earlier conversations, but I don't really know how

16:33.080 --> 16:34.280
to articulate it either.

16:34.280 --> 16:40.240
I mean, one of the problems of dealing with data that is in some senses peculiar to journalism

16:40.240 --> 16:45.120
or is just very commonly found within journalism, if not unique to it, is that you're almost

16:45.120 --> 16:52.280
always starting with some sort of third party data as like your beginning point, like you're

16:52.280 --> 16:57.800
starting with the school scores data that's released by the state of California.

16:57.800 --> 17:02.520
And so there's a series of like cleanup and migration and transformation stuff that you

17:03.520 --> 17:07.840
almost always have to do when you're dealing with government data just to do the analysis

17:07.840 --> 17:11.760
to write the story, let alone to build a new web application based on the data.

17:11.760 --> 17:16.000
And those things just recur and happen in virtually every project.

17:16.000 --> 17:20.720
And now since Django is so easy, I actually spend much more time preparing the data than

17:20.720 --> 17:24.920
I actually do developing it for the website.

17:24.920 --> 17:28.040
And those tasks are things I'm sure people are familiar with, which is just very simple

17:28.040 --> 17:32.840
things often standardization of mutations and parent-child relationships where you

17:32.840 --> 17:37.320
still want to maintain the initial relationship that was in the data as you got it from the

17:37.320 --> 17:38.320
source.

17:38.320 --> 17:44.080
But for the purposes of, say, a web application or analysis, you want to combine two nominative

17:44.080 --> 17:48.760
categories, say, like where in the school's data there's a city field in the address that

17:48.760 --> 17:53.320
the state provides and is commonly filled with misspellings and other problems that

17:53.320 --> 17:57.520
you don't necessarily want to eliminate because you need to maintain the integrity of the

17:57.520 --> 17:59.800
data should you ever need to go back to it.

17:59.800 --> 18:03.600
But you'd like to be able to easily and to be able to do things like that in the admin

18:03.600 --> 18:08.800
where that could be deputized to someone else while the programmer gets to do the programming

18:08.800 --> 18:14.120
stuff rather than going through 60,000 dog breed names like I had to do and get all the

18:14.120 --> 18:17.520
combinations of the things handwritten in the form by the guy who filled it out, you

18:17.520 --> 18:21.200
know, would be a huge boon.

18:21.200 --> 18:25.160
What's the data model like for the dog names thing?

18:25.160 --> 18:29.920
It's actually very simple, the ultimate, what I did is I, since Los Angeles, every

18:29.920 --> 18:32.520
block having just launched in LA, I'm sure you're familiar with some of the problems

18:32.520 --> 18:37.720
of gathering public data in LA and one of them is that the bureaucratic topography,

18:37.720 --> 18:39.920
I guess that you would call it, is just very fragmented.

18:39.920 --> 18:43.840
It's like, so if you're doing every block San Francisco, I mean, there's essentially

18:43.840 --> 18:47.640
one city authority that maintains and centralizes all the stuff.

18:47.640 --> 18:51.720
It's, you know, not unlike a small European country, say, like Sweden or France or something

18:51.720 --> 18:55.840
where you just go to the government office for each thing and get it, but in Los Angeles

18:55.840 --> 18:59.680
you have LA City and you have LA County and you have Santa Monica and you have West Hollywood

18:59.680 --> 19:02.880
and you have Pasadena and you have Long Beach and you have all these places and they all

19:02.880 --> 19:06.800
have their own police departments and they all have their own, like public health is

19:06.800 --> 19:07.800
something that was more centralized.

19:07.800 --> 19:10.680
I think you guys probably had an easier time with that for like restaurant inspection,

19:10.680 --> 19:15.240
but it varies from source to source and so, say, for the dog's data, it was, like when

19:15.240 --> 19:19.880
I first moved to LA, I knew I was going to have to learn all this fragmented bureaucracy,

19:19.880 --> 19:24.200
so let's just start putting in public information requests and try to learn LA County.

19:24.200 --> 19:29.080
And I think that that set ended up being maybe 10 different public information requests that

19:29.080 --> 19:35.040
came back in different CSV files, which I then combined into one large table, which

19:35.040 --> 19:41.400
I did sort of the cleaning and the standardization process on, which then once that was complete,

19:41.400 --> 19:47.680
was migrated into a Django app afterward, but if all that stuff, the standardization

19:47.680 --> 19:53.040
that I ended up having to do through SQL and could have been done within the admin

19:53.040 --> 19:56.600
by someone else, it would have saved a ton of time and labor.

19:56.600 --> 20:00.680
That would be a cool feature, like it's sort of along the same lines as multiple deletion.

20:00.680 --> 20:07.400
It's like multiple changing, like if you have John and Johnny and you want to combine those,

20:07.400 --> 20:11.520
like filter, show me all the John and Johnny and just like, okay, change them all definitively

20:11.520 --> 20:15.960
to John, boom, that would be really, really cool.

20:15.960 --> 20:19.520
That's just welcome.

20:19.520 --> 20:28.000
So Matt, wait, you've on Twitter and on your blog in various places always made self-deprecating.

20:28.000 --> 20:36.200
I'm just a journalist, you know, I'm not a programmer, but Django helps me be productive.

20:36.200 --> 20:40.840
Why do you think Django lets you be productive?

20:41.840 --> 20:49.000
A couple of reasons, first of which being Python, being the first language that I actually

20:49.000 --> 20:53.200
started to learn and actually learned it.

20:53.200 --> 21:00.720
You know, I'm a knuckle-dragging, mouth-breathing, land-grant graduate from the Midwest, liberal

21:00.720 --> 21:08.840
arts major, who, you know, the toughest class I took in college was like, you know, 18th

21:08.840 --> 21:10.880
century English literature.

21:10.880 --> 21:17.280
So I had no experience with programming, got into database journalism like Ben was describing

21:17.280 --> 21:19.440
and I realized, you know, maybe I ought to learn a scripting language.

21:19.440 --> 21:24.720
So people around me that I respected were all talking about Perl, you could do all kinds

21:24.720 --> 21:29.380
of crazy stuff in Perl and I'm like, okay, I'll give Perl a shot and about three days

21:29.380 --> 21:33.120
later I'm like, oh, hell with that.

21:33.120 --> 21:36.200
So then a couple other guys I knew, they were like, yeah, we use PHP to do stuff.

21:36.200 --> 21:39.640
I'm like, okay, I'll try PHP, again, three days later, oh, hell with that.

21:39.640 --> 21:42.120
I'll never get that.

21:42.120 --> 21:46.720
Python was the first language I actually tried and it was like, okay, I want to do this.

21:46.720 --> 21:53.640
And very early on it was like, well, I don't know how to express that.

21:53.640 --> 21:55.000
Maybe it should be like this.

21:55.000 --> 21:57.720
Oh, my God, it worked.

21:57.720 --> 22:02.760
It just, for some reason, kind of the Python syntax is kind of the way I thought it should

22:02.760 --> 22:03.760
be.

22:03.760 --> 22:05.440
And it just did.

22:06.200 --> 22:10.200
A lot of times it was just, I was a little bit off and it was just a process of learning.

22:10.200 --> 22:16.560
But the learning curve for me on Python was a lot, lot less steep than it was for any

22:16.560 --> 22:19.400
of the half a dozen other things I've tried.

22:19.400 --> 22:27.880
Which made Django a lot easier for me because, you know, I know SQL pretty well.

22:27.880 --> 22:35.040
So I could look at view syntax and go, okay, I know what this is doing.

22:35.040 --> 22:38.240
And it just kind of was like, it just kind of went from there.

22:38.240 --> 22:42.840
The discussion earlier, somebody finally put a name to the way that I learned Django, which

22:42.840 --> 22:47.400
was the Z-shaped learning curve, where I ran right up against that wall and bounced off

22:47.400 --> 22:50.560
it hard, kind of went backwards and then started going up again.

22:50.560 --> 22:54.440
And I've described it to other people who are trying to learn Django as kind of a rollercoaster

22:54.440 --> 22:55.440
of learning curves.

22:55.440 --> 22:58.760
You get going up, up, up, up, up, and then you hit the top and you're like, holy crap,

22:58.760 --> 23:00.520
I can do something!

23:00.520 --> 23:05.920
And then you go back up again and you just repeat over and over again until one day you

23:05.920 --> 23:09.880
wake up and you say, oh, I'm going to implement this.

23:09.880 --> 23:13.400
And you implement it and it works just that fast.

23:13.400 --> 23:14.400
Just like that.

23:14.400 --> 23:18.440
It works as you thought about it in your head and you realize, holy crap, the world has

23:18.440 --> 23:20.400
just opened up to me.

23:21.400 --> 23:26.000
That was kind of the process of me learning Django, which is just, I think it should do

23:26.000 --> 23:30.320
this and I think it should be this way and oh my god, it worked that way.

23:30.320 --> 23:34.120
So I don't know, dumb luck, I guess.

23:34.120 --> 23:38.080
I think it's really significant that we've got people on this panel that sort of come

23:38.080 --> 23:39.080
from both sides.

23:39.080 --> 23:43.240
We have journalists that happen to program because of Django and we have programmers

23:43.240 --> 23:47.080
that happen to be into journalism and have learned a lot about journalism because of

23:47.080 --> 23:48.080
Django.

23:48.440 --> 23:52.400
I don't know what it is, but I think it's significant that sort of we've got Django

23:52.400 --> 23:57.840
in the middle bridging these two very, very different sides of what's going on.

23:59.880 --> 24:03.080
Anything else on that topic?

24:03.080 --> 24:10.240
What do you guys absolutely hate about working with Django or at least dislike?

24:10.240 --> 24:13.240
I hate the feeds framework.

24:13.240 --> 24:14.240
The syndication feeds?

24:14.240 --> 24:15.240
Yes.

24:16.240 --> 24:23.360
And we are using 096 at work and we'll upgrade to 1.02 and I know there's been some improvements,

24:23.360 --> 24:27.320
I haven't used it very much, but I mean, have you ever thought of making it more like a

24:27.320 --> 24:31.640
generic view or just, I don't know, more?

24:31.640 --> 24:36.320
It's kind of, I don't know, I hate in the URL comp feed, you kind of like, you kind

24:36.320 --> 24:37.320
of...

24:37.320 --> 24:38.320
Feed dicked.

24:38.320 --> 24:39.320
Feed dicked, yeah.

24:39.320 --> 24:40.320
You don't have to call it that.

24:40.320 --> 24:41.320
You can call it flower dicked.

24:41.320 --> 24:44.960
Well, that's true, but still, nonetheless, it is still the feed dicked.

24:45.360 --> 24:46.360
Okay.

24:47.960 --> 24:51.720
I would say one of the most exciting things that, if I paid closer attention, I probably

24:51.720 --> 24:55.040
already would have known about, but that I saw here this weekend was, I believe it was

24:55.040 --> 24:59.880
called demigrations yesterday, the ability to change your model without eliminating the

24:59.880 --> 25:02.760
data was just something that I constantly ran into.

25:02.760 --> 25:07.960
And if you're learning Django and even learning web development as you kind of do this, as

25:07.960 --> 25:12.800
you go along, I mean, I think both Matt and I come to Django with a very large data set

25:12.800 --> 25:14.800
that you want to get online, you know what I mean?

25:14.800 --> 25:17.520
It's not like a green grass thing where you're going to be building it from scratch.

25:17.520 --> 25:22.240
And so, as you're learning it, you're making mistakes and you're having to mess around

25:22.240 --> 25:24.440
with your models and get things straight.

25:24.440 --> 25:27.840
Having to always like rebuild the data set every time you made that change was something

25:27.840 --> 25:31.760
that was pretty burdensome.

25:31.760 --> 25:34.920
PolitiFact was my first project and I was kind of developing it with the Washington

25:34.920 --> 25:39.880
bureau chief and we were doing a very iterative process, although we didn't know what that

25:39.880 --> 25:40.880
was at the time.

25:40.880 --> 25:42.080
We weren't smart enough to know what it was.

25:42.080 --> 25:46.080
So, he'd call me up and say, okay, hey, I want to capture this information.

25:46.080 --> 25:49.800
So, I'd sketch out a model or a series of models and related tables and I'd send them,

25:49.800 --> 25:52.120
you know, go to the admin and try it out now.

25:52.120 --> 25:56.440
And he'd enter in, actually, he'd report it out, a fact check of something that I believe

25:56.440 --> 26:01.480
Mitt Romney had said and he'd go, okay, well, I really would, I really think we ought to

26:01.480 --> 26:02.480
store this information.

26:02.480 --> 26:06.080
I think we ought to do it or I think we ought to do this other thing and I'm like, okay,

26:06.080 --> 26:07.280
wipe it away.

26:07.280 --> 26:08.560
Start all over again.

26:08.560 --> 26:12.480
And that was fine when it was just one, but then he got kind of excited and started doing

26:12.480 --> 26:16.560
4, 5, 6, and 10 and then asked me to change something else.

26:16.560 --> 26:20.920
And I'm like, you know what's going to happen, right?

26:20.920 --> 26:22.920
He's like, oh.

26:22.920 --> 26:26.600
And I do it anyway.

26:26.600 --> 26:31.880
And you know, I've now reached the stage where the editors are coming in like, can we do

26:31.880 --> 26:32.880
this or that or the other thing?

26:32.880 --> 26:36.640
I'm like, you realize we have like over seven, we have almost 700 statements in the database

26:36.640 --> 26:37.640
right now.

26:37.720 --> 26:38.720
I'm going to mess with it.

26:38.720 --> 26:39.720
I just don't.

26:39.720 --> 26:40.720
I'm too scared.

26:40.720 --> 26:42.640
Just leave it alone.

26:42.640 --> 26:44.160
It works.

26:44.160 --> 26:45.160
Don't jinx it.

26:45.160 --> 26:46.160
All right.

26:46.160 --> 26:47.160
So, yes.

26:47.160 --> 26:50.200
Database migrations, okay.

26:50.200 --> 26:53.840
We actually have a group of Rails developers at the Atlanta Jordan Constitution as well

26:53.840 --> 26:56.320
and as you know, Rails has migrations.

26:56.320 --> 26:59.800
And so, you know, I get to hear that all the time.

26:59.800 --> 27:04.080
So we, we, one of the things I'm actually really interested to look at that I haven't

27:04.160 --> 27:08.240
yet is Simon's thing about migrations because I think it's kind of grown similar to the

27:08.240 --> 27:09.240
way we do.

27:09.240 --> 27:13.600
We have kind of, we do a lot of migrations and they're very, they're just very scary

27:13.600 --> 27:15.960
when you're doing it on a live production database.

27:15.960 --> 27:21.800
And so, you know, we kind of store them or for reference in a Railsian way, like I don't

27:21.800 --> 27:27.720
know if you're familiar, there's like 001 and 002 for your order.

27:27.720 --> 27:29.720
And then, and then we kind of run them and pray.

27:30.720 --> 27:34.720
How many people here have written their own migration stuff for Django?

27:34.720 --> 27:35.720
Impressive.

27:35.720 --> 27:42.960
Matt, what do you hate about that?

27:42.960 --> 27:47.680
I hate to beat a dead horse but, yeah, migrations suck and we need to change them.

27:47.680 --> 27:53.680
What other than migrations?

27:53.680 --> 27:59.360
I think probably the biggest thing that sucks about working with Django is when you have

27:59.360 --> 28:04.600
to work outside of Django, that kind of makes everything else that much more sucky.

28:04.600 --> 28:05.600
Explain that.

28:05.600 --> 28:12.400
Well, you know, so if you spend all day writing Django and then you have to work within, maintain

28:12.400 --> 28:18.720
some old Perl code or maintain something that's not Django, it kind of spoils you for everything

28:18.720 --> 28:20.920
else you have to do.

28:20.920 --> 28:24.800
I got one, aggregate.

28:24.800 --> 28:29.240
Something that Ben and I have done a lot with database journalism is aggregates.

28:29.240 --> 28:33.880
That's pretty much all that you do, is your grouping and counting and summing and sorting

28:33.880 --> 28:36.080
and averaging and all that stuff.

28:36.080 --> 28:42.680
And that was a, you know, a stopping point for me was I got in there and I'm like, okay,

28:42.680 --> 28:50.040
now I want to count all these things and I'm like, where's, you know, where's sales.objects.count?

28:50.040 --> 28:53.440
Or group by this thing, then count them.

28:53.440 --> 28:56.080
And yes, you can drop into SQL and do it.

28:56.080 --> 29:01.840
But boy do I wish there was that one line, group it by this field, count up a number

29:01.840 --> 29:04.400
of unique things within that.

29:04.400 --> 29:07.840
I'm definitely encouraged by the fact that a lot of these things that we're griping about

29:07.840 --> 29:12.080
are things that, you know, if they're not in progress, they're things that we're thinking

29:12.080 --> 29:13.360
about as a community.

29:13.360 --> 29:15.120
So that makes me happy.

29:15.120 --> 29:16.120
Right.

29:16.120 --> 29:22.160
One of the things, maybe some of you feel my pain, but we only work on releases at work.

29:22.160 --> 29:26.680
So we only work on Django 1.6 and now we're going to work on Django 1.0.

29:26.680 --> 29:32.840
And I understand why that wasn't a Django 0.97, but please do not wait so long for your next

29:32.840 --> 29:33.840
version of Django.

29:33.840 --> 29:34.840
Okay.

29:34.840 --> 29:36.920
So that was going to be my very next question.

29:36.920 --> 29:38.320
You mentioned you're on 0.96.

29:38.320 --> 29:41.440
Matt, you guys are on 0.91?

29:41.440 --> 29:43.720
Yeah.

29:43.720 --> 29:46.160
So we're actually not on 0.91.

29:46.160 --> 29:51.880
We've got our internal version of Ellington that isn't commercial, that we just use internally.

29:51.920 --> 29:57.760
It's on revision 12.90 from November 2005.

29:57.760 --> 30:04.680
But we do, we were six failing tests away from being 100% on 1.0 and launching stuff

30:04.680 --> 30:09.080
later this month internally and should have commercial offerings by the end of the next

30:09.080 --> 30:10.720
month or so.

30:10.720 --> 30:15.760
So yeah, that's been, you know, sort of a situation in particular to us that, you know,

30:15.760 --> 30:23.200
I have shiny things in new versions of Django that I can't use yet or have to back port.

30:23.200 --> 30:28.480
That's something that we on the core developer team, you know, just like always run trunk

30:28.480 --> 30:29.480
and everything.

30:29.480 --> 30:33.360
I always run trunk on my little work stuff and projects.

30:33.360 --> 30:34.880
How much of a pain is it?

30:34.880 --> 30:39.120
I mean, you've already kind of talked about it, maybe the other two guys talk about is

30:39.120 --> 30:43.840
it really a huge pain to run on trunk?

30:43.840 --> 30:49.720
In terms of the pointy haired bosses requiring that you run on a release, any experiences

30:49.720 --> 30:50.720
with that?

30:50.720 --> 30:55.080
Yeah, when we set up PolitiFact for the first time, they were scared to death of something

30:55.080 --> 30:56.080
that wasn't 1.0.

30:56.080 --> 31:01.920
And then I said, well, I'd like to run trunk and they went, oh, hell no, oh, hell no.

31:01.920 --> 31:05.040
So PolitiFact runs on 9.6.

31:05.040 --> 31:11.440
Since that actually succeeded and nobody died, you know, the neighborhood watch is actually

31:11.440 --> 31:21.120
running on the GeoJango branch prior to 1.0 and that was actually the SVN version of that.

31:21.120 --> 31:29.120
So we've kind of broken through some barriers here at the times where they were extremely

31:29.120 --> 31:32.200
uncomfortable with things and now it works.

31:32.200 --> 31:36.920
So they're like, as long as you've got a good reason to do it, we can do it.

31:36.920 --> 31:38.120
Let's give it a shot.

31:38.120 --> 31:44.120
So it's, we've, for whatever reason, become more agile when we do that and we're now moving

31:44.120 --> 31:50.920
every, because we have one app running 9.6, we have one app running a branch.

31:50.920 --> 31:55.800
We have to have them on separate servers and we want to put them all together, which 1.0

31:55.800 --> 31:59.680
with the GeoJango Contrib app is a huge deal for us.

31:59.680 --> 32:05.320
So maybe that's one argument for fewer releases is it encourages work environments to be more

32:05.320 --> 32:07.760
agile and run Trunk.

32:07.760 --> 32:12.280
I got to say the last couple of weeks on Trunk have been kind of rough with a really big

32:12.280 --> 32:17.280
code base and, you know, kind of, you're just recovering from the last big backwards incompatible

32:17.280 --> 32:22.080
change when, you know, new comments drops or, you know, old forms goes away and you

32:22.080 --> 32:25.520
realize there's a whole bunch of stuff that you're not actually using but it's still in

32:25.520 --> 32:29.480
your code base and, oh, yeah, you can't load modules anymore.

32:29.480 --> 32:33.920
But yeah, overall, I think Trunk has been pretty stable in the last couple of weeks

32:33.920 --> 32:34.920
withstanding.

32:34.920 --> 32:35.920
Go ahead.

32:35.920 --> 32:42.320
I would say that between 0.096 and 1.0, to answer your original question, yes, it would

32:42.320 --> 32:48.600
have been kind of a painful to run on Trunk at work with, you know, multiple developers.

32:48.600 --> 32:52.520
Even just for me, and this was a little while ago, even just on my personal blog code, I

32:52.520 --> 32:55.920
didn't look at it for a while and I pulled it up and was hacking on it and all of a sudden

32:55.920 --> 32:59.840
form for model was deprecated, you know, that kind of stuff was happening all of the

32:59.840 --> 33:00.840
time recently.

33:00.840 --> 33:04.800
So, yeah, it would have been kind of painful.

33:04.800 --> 33:10.560
I would just say that we're running on 9.6 but our little Django Republic is a guerilla

33:10.560 --> 33:11.560
republic.

33:11.560 --> 33:15.800
I mean, in addition to being the only developer, I guess, it being my education and web development,

33:15.800 --> 33:20.120
it's also been my education server administration where we're working over at Media Temple and

33:20.120 --> 33:27.120
so we haven't had the same problems in that sense, okay.

33:27.120 --> 33:28.120
Time for Q&A.

33:28.120 --> 33:29.120
Any?

33:29.120 --> 33:30.120
Yeah, Russ.

33:30.120 --> 33:31.120
Certainly do.

33:31.120 --> 33:32.760
First one, sort of partial comments.

33:32.760 --> 33:36.480
One, the aggregation is on its way if you've got use cases of interesting type queries.

33:36.480 --> 33:39.000
I want so seriously to get this into 1.1.

33:39.000 --> 33:40.000
God bless you, sir.

33:40.000 --> 33:44.280
The patch is pretty much ready to go minus a couple of little bit of edits and whatnot.

33:44.280 --> 33:49.560
If you have interesting use cases of types of aggregate queries you want to do, please

33:49.560 --> 33:53.440
let us know so we can make sure that we're covering those sort of problems with the

33:53.440 --> 33:56.280
syntax and there aren't any bugs in those sort of areas.

33:56.280 --> 33:58.800
The other one, a quick comment was about evolution.

33:58.800 --> 34:00.800
Again, there's a panelist afternoon.

34:00.800 --> 34:02.160
We're all going to be talking about it.

34:02.160 --> 34:06.680
I'm also a little bit amazed that Simon, both by sheer force of personality and the people

34:06.680 --> 34:10.280
he knows, can release a product three days ago and still get more press than Django Evolution

34:10.280 --> 34:14.680
which has been around for nine months.

34:14.680 --> 34:21.560
The question I wanted to ask was about the Web 2.0, Waves Arms Wildly, and journalism

34:21.560 --> 34:26.640
and the attitude of the organisations you work with and the less enlightened who haven't

34:26.640 --> 34:31.600
quite drunkled the Kool-Aid yet in terms of mash-ups and pulling information together

34:31.600 --> 34:32.600
and whatnot.

34:32.600 --> 34:37.080
You're all with traditional media organisations doing traditional newspapers and that sort

34:37.080 --> 34:42.760
of thing and you have got journalists presumably not going out and individually interviewing

34:42.760 --> 34:46.000
dog owners to find out what type of dog they've got, they're scraping.

34:46.000 --> 34:49.240
There are still newspapers out there that are very much just printing and putting ink

34:49.240 --> 34:53.800
on paper and you end up with things like the AP coming out and saying, no, you can't quote

34:53.800 --> 34:59.280
more than four words on your website without paying a licence fee.

34:59.280 --> 35:02.960
What's the best way to educate the, I mean, it's interesting to me because I work for

35:02.960 --> 35:09.840
a company that is doing this sort of mash-up integration and doing search, passing links

35:09.840 --> 35:15.880
back to traditional media organisations who aren't necessarily down with what we're doing

35:15.880 --> 35:18.160
with their content.

35:18.160 --> 35:23.480
How do we as a community and just as individuals convince them that this is in their benefit

35:23.480 --> 35:29.920
and it's in their interest to be into this mass linking, mass building value by putting

35:29.920 --> 35:33.880
the links between data?

35:33.880 --> 35:39.760
Here and simply, the economic terms, particularly here in the United States, newspapers are

35:39.760 --> 35:44.760
hurting bad and if you can go into a newspaper manager and say, if you let us do this, you'll

35:44.760 --> 35:47.560
make money, done.

35:47.560 --> 35:50.560
You don't have to say anything else after that.

35:50.560 --> 35:55.320
I may be slightly oversimplifying it but Ben and I would probably agree with this, not

35:55.320 --> 35:58.320
that much.

35:58.320 --> 36:04.760
The newspaper business model online right now is selling advertisers the most possible

36:04.760 --> 36:08.360
eyeballs in a given day.

36:08.360 --> 36:11.600
They're selling advertising per thousand page views.

36:11.600 --> 36:15.600
So if you can show them what you're doing is going to increase the number of page views,

36:15.600 --> 36:18.480
they're not going to care at all.

36:18.480 --> 36:22.720
It means money in their pocket and right now, every newspaper company in America could use

36:22.720 --> 36:25.280
every dollar in their pocket they can get.

36:25.280 --> 36:28.600
So that's the approach I would take.

36:28.600 --> 36:34.960
Crass, yes, not very idealistic, obviously, but it will work.

36:34.960 --> 36:37.600
It will work very quickly.

36:37.600 --> 36:45.800
And a lot of particularly senior newspaper executives, that's what they'll understand.

36:45.800 --> 36:51.840
This whole idea of sharing information and extracting more meaning out of disparate data

36:51.840 --> 36:58.280
and somehow coming to some greater understanding, it goes right over their head.

36:58.280 --> 37:02.560
Say you're going to make more money if you let us do this, done.

37:02.560 --> 37:05.360
Any ideas on how to get their ears in the first place?

37:05.360 --> 37:10.320
Well, Adrian, you just had some success getting every block on the Chicago Tribune's website

37:10.320 --> 37:12.840
and the Chicago Sun-Times website, right?

37:12.840 --> 37:15.920
How did that happen?

37:15.920 --> 37:20.720
So it's sort of a special case because I knew some people there and they're interested in

37:20.720 --> 37:21.720
the project.

37:21.720 --> 37:26.640
There was some knowledge of the fact that the project existed.

37:26.640 --> 37:34.520
In my own experience, it's frustrating and it's difficult, and I've just had some lucky

37:34.520 --> 37:38.920
chances to work in places where there's been some clued in people.

37:38.920 --> 37:40.920
All right, Jason.

37:40.920 --> 37:44.120
You want to go first?

37:44.120 --> 37:45.120
You're first.

37:45.120 --> 37:46.120
All right.

37:46.120 --> 37:49.120
So just backing up what Adrian was saying earlier about journalists being some of the

37:49.120 --> 37:52.360
most technologically backwards people you can possibly work with.

37:52.360 --> 37:58.080
I work at a school of journalism and you want to sell them on this Django thing and they're

37:58.080 --> 38:01.920
going to come in and say they have to type in an href equals blah, blah, blah, and they're

38:01.920 --> 38:04.040
going to run screaming back to WordPress.

38:04.040 --> 38:07.680
So implementing without TinyMCE is kind of not an option.

38:07.680 --> 38:11.320
If you want to do these four-hour projects and you sort of got to go get the project

38:11.320 --> 38:15.560
and get it all installed, the way I'd love to see things work would be one line in the

38:15.560 --> 38:22.320
settings file would pull out a contrib thing and every text field would just have it.

38:22.320 --> 38:28.160
But part two to that is not just kind of a TinyMCE that we see, but a toggleable field

38:28.160 --> 38:32.280
so you can toggle between visual and HTML for when it screws up and have it nice and

38:32.280 --> 38:33.280
clean.

38:33.280 --> 38:36.000
So, you know, look to recent versions of WordPress, they do that really cleanly.

38:36.000 --> 38:42.840
Yeah, I don't let people use HTML because they know a little bit of HTML would be just

38:42.880 --> 38:46.760
enough to be dangerous.

38:46.760 --> 38:53.240
Yeah, I think that the right place to put that would be in the new admin classes.

38:53.240 --> 38:58.000
Use MCE equals true or, you know, whatever in order to automatically do it for every

38:58.000 --> 38:59.000
text field.

38:59.000 --> 39:00.000
Oh, yeah.

39:00.000 --> 39:04.240
I mean, I've done TinyMCE in Django, it's pretty easy.

39:04.240 --> 39:09.280
And doesn't TinyMCE have a thing where it can toggle itself, like within its...

39:09.280 --> 39:13.080
The default version, if you switch out to HTML mode, it brings up a separate window

39:13.080 --> 39:16.480
and you get this tangle of spaghetti code.

39:16.480 --> 39:20.960
If you look at the equivalent thing in WordPress, you just get nice clean text with just the

39:20.960 --> 39:23.840
minimal amount of formatting.

39:23.840 --> 39:24.840
Wishlist.

39:24.840 --> 39:25.840
Cool.

39:25.840 --> 39:28.600
Yeah, over there.

39:28.600 --> 39:33.200
This isn't really a Django thing, it's more content type inside of a content management

39:33.200 --> 39:39.720
system inside of a traditional newspaper, something I've been obsessed with since probably

39:39.720 --> 39:42.000
my second year of college.

39:42.000 --> 39:49.000
How do you take categories like front section, local, national, sports, and then push that

39:50.400 --> 39:55.200
onto a better situation on the web?

39:55.200 --> 40:00.360
So you have items like stories, multimedia, video, and then you have categories tagging

40:00.360 --> 40:01.880
and everything like that.

40:02.080 --> 40:06.760
How do you end up keeping everything flat enough so that way it makes sense to the traditional

40:06.760 --> 40:12.800
editors but yet makes sense inside the database as well?

40:12.800 --> 40:15.200
I think the honest answer is no one's really figured that out yet.

40:15.200 --> 40:19.760
I mean, pull up any newspaper.com and ask yourself, did they really figure that one

40:19.760 --> 40:20.760
out?

40:20.760 --> 40:23.920
And I think the answer is no.

40:23.920 --> 40:29.040
I heard somebody describe newspaper websites as being a giant ball of mud that we're just

40:29.040 --> 40:33.800
packing on more stuff and it's just accumulating over time until it just grows and grows and

40:33.800 --> 40:35.960
grows.

40:35.960 --> 40:42.560
So maybe Matt has a better answer than I do, but from what I can see, no one's answered

40:42.560 --> 40:44.520
that question satisfactorily yet.

40:44.520 --> 40:47.160
If they do, they're going to make a shitload of money.

40:47.160 --> 40:52.520
Yeah, I mean, on the technical side, categorize everything, tag everything, do it in a generic

40:52.520 --> 40:58.160
way, and then build some utilities that help you pull stuff out.

40:58.160 --> 41:04.240
I mean, we've got some stuff in Ellington that makes it easy to sort of bring up, show

41:04.240 --> 41:08.920
me all of the content for this section, show all of the content for this category.

41:08.920 --> 41:13.520
And it really only works that way because everything's categorized and once you talk

41:13.520 --> 41:20.800
about user-generated tagging, everything's tagged, aggregate that stuff and do good visualizations

41:20.800 --> 41:25.800
on that.

41:25.800 --> 41:31.640
I just quickly want to say thanks to Ben and his team for the best internet black hole

41:31.640 --> 41:36.600
of the last two weeks in the dog project.

41:36.600 --> 41:37.600
What's your dog's name?

41:37.600 --> 41:38.600
Scruffy.

41:38.600 --> 41:39.600
Scruffy.

41:39.600 --> 41:41.200
And how popular is Scruffy?

41:41.200 --> 41:45.800
I didn't put him in because you guys just included LA County dogs.

41:45.800 --> 41:49.400
We're unfortunately just south of the border by like 300 yards.

41:49.400 --> 41:50.400
You're an OC?

41:50.400 --> 41:51.400
Yeah.

41:51.400 --> 41:52.400
Okay.

41:52.400 --> 41:53.400
Well, thanks for using the site.

41:53.400 --> 41:54.400
I appreciate it.

41:54.400 --> 41:55.400
It was fabulous.

41:56.400 --> 42:05.400
I have a real quick thing to talk a little bit about the syndication framework.

42:05.400 --> 42:11.080
Actually, if you dig into it just a little bit, you'll notice there's a views.py file

42:11.080 --> 42:13.920
and it literally is just a generic view.

42:13.920 --> 42:20.140
So outside of any problem with regards to how you deal with the like data and making

42:20.140 --> 42:26.780
nicer feeds and whatever, if you're frustrated with how it forces you to deal with like

42:26.780 --> 42:31.780
where data's coming through in my projects, personally, I've just ripped out that view

42:31.780 --> 42:34.940
and I wrap it in other views and just throw it around anywhere.

42:34.940 --> 42:35.940
Yeah.

42:35.940 --> 42:40.220
I guess my beef is really that the way, the default way that you're supposed to use it

42:40.220 --> 42:41.220
that's presented as a bit and ideal.

42:41.220 --> 42:42.220
Right.

42:42.220 --> 42:44.220
Default way is just totally broken.

42:44.220 --> 42:49.620
One more question.

42:49.620 --> 42:52.460
This isn't necessarily Django related, but I was wondering if the four people on the

42:52.460 --> 42:58.200
panel could talk a little bit more about kind of the newspaper's decline that we're reading

42:58.200 --> 43:02.580
a lot about and how you guys see just technology and, I mean, you guys are kind of on the front

43:02.580 --> 43:03.580
lines of that.

43:03.580 --> 43:08.340
So what do you see it and how do you see it and I'd love to hear about that.

43:08.340 --> 43:09.340
I think-

43:09.340 --> 43:10.340
In 15 words or less.

43:10.340 --> 43:11.340
In 15 words or less.

43:11.340 --> 43:16.540
I think that Matt kind of really touched on what it's all about right now is page views

43:16.540 --> 43:19.380
equals dollars, which is unfortunate.

43:19.460 --> 43:26.700
I think that has some negative effects on journalism because what people want to see

43:26.700 --> 43:31.660
or read is maybe not the most, you know, high-brow journalism.

43:31.660 --> 43:34.380
I mean, people love pictures of celebrities.

43:34.380 --> 43:39.060
We have lots of pictures of celebrities and they get lots of page views, which is dollars,

43:39.060 --> 43:41.460
but I don't really consider that journalism.

43:41.460 --> 43:42.460
Yeah.

43:42.460 --> 43:48.900
If you would have found us at the bar last night, Ben and I were drinking and talking

43:48.900 --> 43:53.220
about this in that particular order.

43:53.220 --> 43:57.980
I think what Ben and I want to believe and what I think some of the things that we're

43:57.980 --> 44:03.340
doing and the things that Adrian are doing is that we are hoping against hope that a

44:03.340 --> 44:10.020
new model, a new business model for journalism can arise from some of the things that we're

44:10.020 --> 44:15.500
thinking about and doing and trying out and experimenting with that gets away from this.

44:15.500 --> 44:24.660
The more eyeballs wins idea that if you can sell an advertiser a specific piece of geography

44:24.660 --> 44:30.220
that they happen to that, you know, 80, 90% of their customer base is in, that should

44:30.220 --> 44:36.540
have value to them beyond just a thousand random page views that could come from anywhere

44:36.540 --> 44:37.980
in the world.

44:37.980 --> 44:45.340
So while as journalists, there's this Chinese wall between the business side and the

44:45.340 --> 44:51.180
journalism side, so I can't just walk into the advertising side and go, hey, let's tell

44:51.180 --> 44:54.580
me how to make money and let's just start doing it.

44:54.580 --> 44:58.620
But I can talk to my bosses and they can talk to their counterparts and we can start trying

44:58.620 --> 45:00.540
to push these ideas.

45:00.540 --> 45:07.020
It's way too early to know if that's actually going to succeed, if the bottom won't fall

45:07.020 --> 45:11.460
out long before it does, but that's not to say that there aren't people trying to work

45:11.460 --> 45:17.540
on it, that there are good and useful and not involving Lindsay Lohan things that we

45:17.540 --> 45:26.180
can do to inform our citizenry, inform democracy, not to get too high headed here about it,

45:26.180 --> 45:33.100
but that should have value and we need to build a business model around that.

45:33.100 --> 45:34.940
The problem is it's not there yet.

45:34.940 --> 45:36.420
It's not close.

45:37.220 --> 45:43.460
Yeah, I think working within the existing page views equals dollars framework that while

45:43.460 --> 45:46.140
potentially broken is what we've got right now.

45:46.140 --> 45:51.100
I think one thing you can really do is if you're doing those investigative journalism,

45:51.100 --> 45:57.220
data visualization projects, make it freaking compelling and make it really relevant to

45:57.220 --> 45:58.220
your audience.

45:58.220 --> 46:04.500
I mean, you can take something from a national data set and put a local spin on it and another

46:04.500 --> 46:08.900
thing you can do is get something that is continually updated, something that people

46:08.900 --> 46:16.460
will come back to and that'll let you sustain the financial side of it and so hopefully

46:16.460 --> 46:22.020
do enough of those and people will stop asking you how your journalism is making money.

46:22.020 --> 46:23.620
Is that a problem?

46:23.620 --> 46:29.540
One other thing to that that I learned particularly with doing PolitiFact and kind of increasing

46:29.540 --> 46:34.340
the amount of traffic that you get to your site is, you know, PolitiFact is my first

46:34.340 --> 46:35.340
project.

46:35.340 --> 46:39.460
I never had to worry about, you know, Google search engine optimization, but I learned

46:39.460 --> 46:44.500
and I put it in there and that's now paying off hugely and that 80 plus percent of the

46:44.500 --> 46:48.780
traffic that we get comes in sideways, it doesn't come through the main page.

46:48.780 --> 46:53.820
I learned how to make widgets, embeddable widgets that other people can embed on their blogs

46:53.820 --> 46:57.300
and their sites and you can put our content any old place you want because the links come

46:57.300 --> 46:59.460
back to us.

46:59.460 --> 47:02.420
A Twitter feed, what else have we done?

47:02.420 --> 47:09.420
Google custom homepage gadgets, any, I just did an iPhone, a quick and dirty iPhone thing

47:09.420 --> 47:14.620
because we found out that PolitiFact gets like 40 percent of all of the mobile traffic

47:14.620 --> 47:20.020
that any of my newspaper company sites get and 85 percent of that traffic is iPhones.

47:20.020 --> 47:24.540
So hell with it, I'm making me an iPhone app, took me a couple of hours of the, by the way,

47:24.540 --> 47:30.460
if anybody's trying to do this, IUI, which is a JavaScript and CSS library is awesome.

47:30.460 --> 47:33.060
That's my short commercial because I used it.

47:33.060 --> 47:37.980
One of the interesting things about, I'm totally ripping this off from my boss, Chris Heisel,

47:37.980 --> 47:43.900
thank you, about online journalism versus print journalism is in print, when you bought

47:43.900 --> 47:47.420
the paper, you would get, you know, X number of stories.

47:47.420 --> 47:51.540
You might read some of them, you might not, but all the stories were subsidized by the

47:51.540 --> 47:52.740
fact that you bought the paper.

47:52.740 --> 47:53.820
It's like a package deal.

47:53.820 --> 47:55.780
It's like buying an album.

47:55.780 --> 48:00.260
But now with online journalism, we can see what the stories that readers are actually

48:00.260 --> 48:04.420
interested in and so we can no longer subsidize all the stories in that way.

48:04.420 --> 48:07.980
It's like buying a single track off iTunes.

48:07.980 --> 48:11.140
Just to dovetail on that, I mean, if people in here are in the situation where they want

48:11.140 --> 48:15.220
to pitch something to news managers or to people in these organizations, I think one

48:15.220 --> 48:19.060
of the things he touched on will get you a lot of traction, which is that of search engine

48:19.060 --> 48:23.380
optimization, which is, I think, is something that Django can actually deliver on and that

48:23.380 --> 48:28.740
will provide something greatly superior to virtually any newspaper CMS that's sort

48:28.740 --> 48:30.620
of on the market.

48:30.620 --> 48:34.100
Because if you just go to the LA Times site right now and click on maybe the main story

48:34.100 --> 48:37.580
and look what that role looks like and then ask yourself how well that's going to do in

48:37.580 --> 48:41.300
a search engine and compare that to what you can say to them and you can give them the

48:41.300 --> 48:45.060
buzz word and you can then actually, I think, deliver the results.

48:45.060 --> 48:48.940
From the people, the managers I work with, I think that's a pitch that will resonate

48:48.940 --> 48:53.340
and that will click.

48:53.340 --> 48:57.820
Designing your own URLs is Google search engine observation out of the box.

48:57.820 --> 49:02.740
Clean semantic URLs is one of the things you can do and it's just, it's baked in.

49:02.740 --> 49:03.740
You just have to do it.

49:03.740 --> 49:08.820
Yeah, I like to say that Django is SEO friendly by default for free.

49:08.820 --> 49:14.780
How much time do we have, by the way?

49:14.780 --> 49:17.900
I have one quick question that that reminded me of.

49:17.900 --> 49:23.780
On our Django project.com homepage, we have, you know, the things that we advertise quote

49:23.780 --> 49:24.780
unquote.

49:24.780 --> 49:28.220
We come with an admin framework, we come with internationalization, we come with a template

49:28.220 --> 49:29.940
language.

49:29.940 --> 49:34.900
It sounds as if you guys are saying we should advertise some slightly different things or

49:34.900 --> 49:36.820
additional things.

49:36.820 --> 49:40.900
Do you think that advertising the SEO is something I've never even thought of?

49:40.900 --> 49:45.340
Do you think that it's worth advertising that at that level or is there anything else

49:45.340 --> 49:46.900
that we should advertise at that level?

49:46.900 --> 49:51.580
It's kind of amazing to me that people make a bunch of money doing SEO consulting.

49:51.580 --> 49:56.620
Not that people don't need it, but the fact of the matter is you just need a good URL

49:56.620 --> 49:59.780
and a good title tag and Django encourages that.

49:59.780 --> 50:05.100
So, I mean, I think that, you know, like I said, I think Django encourages you to be

50:05.100 --> 50:06.100
SEO friendly.

50:06.100 --> 50:09.740
I think it's also ultimately dependent on the audience that you're aiming at.

50:09.740 --> 50:15.980
If the Django project page is aimed at developers as a whole, maybe, maybe not.

50:15.980 --> 50:22.020
If it's aimed at IT executives who are debating whether or not they want to buy in and deploy

50:22.020 --> 50:26.500
Django in their shop, yeah, I definitely would put it on there.

50:26.500 --> 50:30.420
And if I was trying to sell Ellington, I definitely would mention that.

50:30.420 --> 50:32.460
I think we go.

50:32.460 --> 50:33.460
Okay.

50:33.460 --> 50:34.460
One more.

50:34.460 --> 50:38.060
I'm wondering whether anyone thinks that newspapers really care about the, like, dead

50:38.060 --> 50:43.300
tree archives that they've got the last 150 years because I've written a, you know, newspaper

50:43.300 --> 50:48.980
viewer that just, you stick in microfilm images and out comes sort of a dirty OCR newspaper

50:48.980 --> 50:49.980
site.

50:49.980 --> 50:53.300
But all the stuff that I've seen is just like, oh, you can pay for access.

50:53.300 --> 50:56.700
And, you know, nobody looks at that because they don't know what's in there in the first

50:56.700 --> 50:57.700
place.

50:57.700 --> 50:58.700
Well, you know, you'd be surprised.

50:58.700 --> 51:03.460
At the AJC, we have that you can pay for old access to the old archives.

51:03.460 --> 51:06.420
And the only reason it's still pay is because people pay for it.

51:06.420 --> 51:07.820
So it does happen.

51:07.820 --> 51:11.860
So they care deeply about their archives because they try to make money off of them.

51:11.860 --> 51:16.820
Unfortunately, well, fortunately, the company I work for actually, the archives are open

51:16.820 --> 51:18.820
going back to the 80s.

51:18.820 --> 51:24.740
The unfortunate side of that is that it's in the world's most hideous search interface

51:24.740 --> 51:28.980
that makes it impossibly ugly to deal with.

51:28.980 --> 51:31.420
Newspaper archives are actually big money.

51:31.420 --> 51:37.460
Newspapers sell their content to, like, LexisNexis to make a commercial aggregation company

51:37.460 --> 51:40.460
that other researchers use around the company or country.

51:40.460 --> 51:48.220
So what I love to see us doing a hell of a lot more with our archives, absolutely,

51:48.220 --> 51:53.260
because if you think about it, your local newspaper is the best, most consistent archive

51:53.260 --> 51:57.180
of the history of your community going.

51:57.180 --> 52:02.740
No matter what you think of your hometown newspaper, there's nothing better when looking

52:02.740 --> 52:08.220
at the history of your community than your newspaper, good or bad as it may be.

52:08.220 --> 52:14.140
So I think there's incredible value, incredible long tail value in the archives going way,

52:14.140 --> 52:15.300
way back.

52:15.300 --> 52:19.020
The problem is that a lot of newspapers are just trying to stay above water right now

52:19.020 --> 52:20.860
and keep up with things going on the web.

52:20.860 --> 52:23.900
They're not looking backwards at the stuff they've got.

52:23.900 --> 52:29.060
We're too busy going forward to know where we've been.

52:29.060 --> 52:34.740
One thing I'll note, we don't have all of our print archives going back, you know, till

52:34.740 --> 52:38.620
the dawn of time, though we probably should.

52:38.620 --> 52:44.220
We notice that we get a lot of search traffic and a lot of eyeballs because our archives

52:44.220 --> 52:45.700
are open.

52:45.700 --> 52:51.700
And so from a sort of a business standpoint, I think we make more money on advertisement

52:51.700 --> 52:56.140
because we have, you know, you can go back to the early 90s and everything we've published

52:56.140 --> 53:01.620
online is there and it's not behind a registration wall or a paywall, it's just there, take it.

53:01.620 --> 53:04.660
And I think we get more page views coming in via Google because of that.

53:04.660 --> 53:05.660
Sure.

53:05.660 --> 53:07.780
And I think there's also a search for monetization out of those as well.

53:07.780 --> 53:10.540
I mean, the New York Times has been a leader in doing this where they've put up a large

53:10.540 --> 53:14.660
portion of their archives and some, even the front pages and things like that.

53:14.660 --> 53:19.540
And I think it's just a matter of the technology being in place for a service like ProQuest,

53:19.540 --> 53:23.220
if you've ever seen ProQuest where you can go and I think on our site you can find an

53:23.220 --> 53:27.500
article in the archives and then purchase it, but you never actually get to see a preview

53:27.500 --> 53:28.500
of it.

53:28.500 --> 53:31.420
So like you're paying five bucks without even seeing what the page looks like.

53:31.420 --> 53:36.580
And I think that the modernization of that technology where you could maybe have just

53:36.580 --> 53:42.580
the printed text in, you know, HTML of say an old story, but maybe you throw down five

53:42.580 --> 53:46.460
bucks to get an image file of what that page looked like or that editorial cartoon that

53:46.460 --> 53:52.820
ran that day or that obituary for your grandfather or any of that stuff, I think that there probably

53:52.820 --> 53:55.180
is a good deal of long tail potential for that.

53:55.180 --> 54:00.100
It's just old photos even, you know, it's just a matter of getting it out online.

54:00.100 --> 54:03.840
But I think that, especially the New York Times leadership and I think the general sort

54:03.840 --> 54:08.740
of maximum page view sort of world view has led people to see that there's at least some

54:08.740 --> 54:13.780
